              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for to give reasons for, then the German for the justification."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: nämlich",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nämlich — \"you see (adding the reason); namely\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ich komme heute nicht. Ich bin nämlich krank. I am not coming today. I am ill, you see."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nämlich adds the reason after the fact and stands after the verb, not at the start: Ich bin nämlich krank. It also means namely: zwei Farben, nämlich Rot und Blau."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nämlich goes back to Middle High German nemelīch \"by name, expressly\", built on Name \"name\". EtymDB has no reliable entry for the adverb, so this follows the usual account: first you named the thing, later the reason."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
